{% if team %}
## Agent Teams

Agent Teams are enabled (`CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS=1`, mode `{{ team_mode }}`).
For work that benefits from parallel exploration, spawn a team instead of delegating
sequentially: run `/team <task>`, or ask in natural language — e.g. "spawn 3 teammates to
review this from security, performance and test-coverage angles". Each teammate reuses one
of the agent roles above, runs in its own context, and coordinates through the shared task
list and direct messages.

- Give each teammate an independent slice; don't let two edit the same files.
- Put read-only exploration (web pages, PDFs, code scans) on parallel teammates; serialize only on real dependencies.
- 3–5 teammates is the sweet spot; teams use more tokens than a single session.
- Experimental. `teammateMode` is set in this project's `settings.json`, but it's a
  user-level preference — move it to `~/.claude/settings.json` if it isn't honored here.
{% if plan_gate or risk_rounds or confidence_threshold or approval_gate or check_cmd %}
### Governed workflow

This team runs under governance gates. The team keeps its working state under `.claude/team/`:
`plan.md` (the shared task list, intent, and risk register). These are created at runtime — don't
pre-author them.
{% if plan_gate %}
- **Plan approval first.** Run `/team-plan` before executing: interview, draft the shared task
  list, present it, and take questions and requested improvements until the user approves. The
  `TaskCreated` hook blocks execution tasks until `.claude/team/plan.md` contains `Status: APPROVED`.
  An approval holds for that plan and that team run only: editing the plan, or a later session,
  makes it stale until the user approves again.
{%- endif %}
{% if confidence_threshold %}
- **Confidence gate.** Finish each task with a last message that ends in a line
  `Confidence: NN%` (e.g. `Confidence: 97%`); the `TaskCompleted` hook blocks completion below
  ≥ {{ confidence_threshold }}%. It reads the confidence from your last message — never from the
  task's subject or description, which the lead wrote — or, if that has none, from
  `.claude/team/confidence/<team-name>/<task-id>.txt` (a block names the exact path; the file is
  removed once the task passes). A teammate may not pick up the next task until the current one
  clears the gate.
{%- endif %}
{%- if check_cmd %}

- **The task check.** The `TaskCompleted` hook runs `{{ check_cmd }}` in the shared directory,
  one teammate at a time, so it can fail on another teammate's unfinished edit — if a failure
  isn't yours, say so instead of editing their files. A task may narrow the check with a line
  `Check: {{ check_cmd }} <arguments>` in its description; any other `Check:` line is ignored.
{%- endif %}
{% if risk_rounds %}
- **Risk mitigation rounds.** Write each risk on one line with an `Owner: <teammate-name>` (the
  name the teammate was spawned with, e.g. `implementer-2`) and a `Mitigation: pending` status;
  leave it pending until addressed, then mark it `done` or `accepted`. The `TeammateIdle` hook
  holds a teammate only while a risk **it owns** is still pending — so a teammate that closed its
  own risks idles freely even while others' remain open, and it is never pressured to fabricate
  closures for risks it doesn't own. A role as owner (`Owner: implementer`) holds no named
  teammate, since several may share a role — the hook reports it instead. Read-only roles (no
  Write/Edit tool) are exempt.
{%- endif %}
{% if loop_guard_max %}
- **Loop guard.** No gate holds a teammate forever: after {{ loop_guard_max }} blocks on the same
  task, role or action (or when its stated confidence stops rising), confidence and risk gates
  release it with the result marked **UNRESOLVED**, while the plan and approval gates stay closed
  and tell it to stop retrying. Each trip is logged to `.claude/loop-guard/escalations.md` — the
  lead reports those as unresolved.
{%- endif %}
{% if approval_gate %}
- **Execution-approval gate.** No agent may perform a high-impact external action — `ssh`,
  cloud mutations (`aws`/`gcloud`/`az`), `git push`/`merge`, `gh pr merge`/`release`,
  `terraform apply`, `kubectl`/`helm`, deploys, or publishes — until a **human** reviews and
  approves. Approving is for a human, from their own terminal: `ocgen approve` (it lasts 30
  minutes by default and re-locks by itself). Never try to approve, never touch
  `~/.claude/ocgen/`, and never phrase a command to get past the gate. A `PreToolUse` hook
  matches the command text and refuses these; with the sandbox on, sandboxed commands can't write
  the approval store and run without the credentials ocgen withholds. A git `pre-push` hook is a
  backstop (`--no-verify` skips it); branch protection on the server is what really stops a push.
  Plan and local edits are allowed before the line; when you reach it, stop and ask the human to
  review and approve.
{%- endif %}
